[{:seon.task/name "a-run-past-its-cap-ends"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen in the related A/B (worktree a-text-written-on-a-branch-is-embedded-there, round on-2, 2026-10-25, deepseek, :seon.config.agent/max-turns 30): the run of the task an-unrun-proof-is-unknown-in-the-context recorded 29 screen evals and 36 model evals, no :seon.end fact, status :running and its agent :busy for over an hour after its last turn, with an empty stderr (runs/store-agents/main/a1.err, 0 bytes); its root answered every other request. The round's stats are docs/vector.md; the store was erased with the round (an experiment store), so what the agent was evaluating is not kept. Not known: whether the last eval hung (a form with no bound; every form has :seon.config.agent/form-ticks now), the model call never answered (seon.model's edge, no timeout by design: a never-answered call is a recorded stall, AGENTS §9), or the loop missed its cap. The first step reproduces it with the scripted provider at the cap (a reply whose eval never returns; a call never answered) and reads the agent's state over the server while it is busy (seon_agents, the agent's :fx-pending, its last eval record), so the stall is a fact on the run (blocked, stalled) and the run ends rather than holding its agent: never a clock deciding; the fact names what it waits on."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-run-waiting-past-its-cap-says-on-what}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-outside-context-is-the-turns-context"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.turn/context-block` (the server's read of a task for an outside worktree session) now takes the run's plan and `seon.turn/observe`'s gaps since the branch's merge base (`seon.merge/uncontracted`), as the turn loop does, so main's pre-existing contract gaps are no longer charged to the agent. It still passes turn n of max-turns 1 to `seon.turn/basis!`, so the context says \"turn 1 of 1\" while the run's env says 8. Correct: context-block passes the run's env max-turns, so the outside session reads exactly what the turn loop's model would. Replaces the second set of basis arguments; nothing new.\n\nThe proof, in test/seon/turn_test.clj, red on main for this reason: the read's recorded basis has :max-turns 1 while the run's max-turns is 40 (it needs [seon.agent-test :as at] in that ns's :require; a process test because context-block needs an attached agent; it keeps the named proof's name, though it asserts the max-turns half of the task: the gaps half is already built). Add it as written, then make it pass.\n```clojure\n(deftest an-outside-context-names-only-the-branchs-own-gaps\n  ;; task seon.turn/an-outside-context-is-the-turns-context (docs/server.md#working-a-task-from-a-worktree): the read an\n  ;; outside session makes (seon.turn/context-block) is the request the turn loop would draw, so its basis (recorded on\n  ;; the branch as the read's model call) holds the run's max-turns, as a model turn's does. A process test: the read\n  ;; makes the process work the task as run, which only an attached agent in a root does (seon.agent-test/in-agent).\n  (let [[_ bases max-turns] (at/in-agent (fn [{:keys [agent]}]\n                                           [{:op :context :agent agent}\n                                            \"(mapv (fn [m] (:seon.model-call/basis (datahike.api/pull (seon.agent/db) [:seon.model-call/basis] m))) (map first (datahike.api/q '[:find ?m :where [?m :seon.model-call/origin :outside]] (seon.agent/db))))\"\n                                            \"(seon.config/value :seon.config.agent/max-turns)\"]))]\n    (is (= [(:value max-turns)] (mapv :max-turns (:value bases)))\n        (str \"the read's basis names the run's max-turns, not 1: \" (pr-str (:value bases))))))\n```"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/an-outside-context-names-only-the-branchs-own-gaps}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-branch-asks-whether-a-test-is-a-process-test"
  :seon.task/plan "Dogfood 0.7, form: (seon.agent/branch-graph) at the REPL is an arity error (it takes db, base db and inputs), and no public my.agent function answers \"is this test a process test on my branch\", which decides whether (my.agent/prove) runs it or defers it. Add that answer to my.agent beside tests-reaching and deferred (status of a test: :process, :plain), reading the reach graph the test plan reads. Proof: (my.agent/tests-reaching ..) results carry :process? for a test reaching a process var."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/tests-reaching-says-which-tests-are-process-tests}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-that-breaks-its-own-render-is-told-and-goes-on"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-02 by the context lab's curation pilot (a scripted replay of task 618a903ed3 on a reverse-patched tree; repro in /private/tmp/seon-l2r/618a903ed3/repro-no-end/README.md): turn 3 made three calls; edit! of seon.ctx/members-map was refused (its old text ended in a newline the recorded form lacks), the next call's edit! of seon.ctx/join applied anyway (join now calls (members-map pairs flag)), and the agent's next context render threw; its following turn threw the same way and the run ended :no-end, \"two turns in a row threw\" (seon.turn/work!), the error never reaching the model. Two causes, each general: (1) a call whose answer is an error value (a refused edit!, any :seon.error map) does not stop the reply's later calls, though `seon.turn/act!` stops them after a throw, so a later call builds on a change that did not happen; (2) an agent that breaks code its own turn loop runs (seon.ctx, the render, seon.context: Seon improves itself) can no longer be sent anything, since the request is drawn by that code, so 'a turn that threw is told' cannot tell it. Correct: (1) a call answering an error value stops the reply's later calls as a throw does (seon.repl/unrun's not-run, the same one result); (2) a turn whose request cannot be drawn sends the previous request as it was sent (the prefix the cache already holds, seon.context/request) with the error as one message printed by the one printer, so the agent reads why and fixes its branch; the run ends only by the existing caps (max-turns), never because the agent's own code broke its page. Owner's ruling 2026-10-28 (never crash, never hang: errors go to whoever can fix them). Design (2) to be shown to the owner before it is built."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/an-error-answer-stops-the-replys-later-calls seon.turn-test/a-turn-whose-page-cannot-be-drawn-sends-its-error}}]
